window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"chem","n":"Chemistry","tab":"Chemistry","w":"Matter and solutions, reactions, and chemistry in daily life.","topics":[{"id":"matter","n":"Matter, Atoms and Solutions","qs":[{"id":"q0276","q":"Which of the following is an example of a colloidal solution commonly found in the kitchen?","o":["Salt dissolved in water","Coffee","Oil and vinegar in salad dressing","Sugar dissolved in water"],"a":1,"x":"DSSSB's key takes brewed coffee as the colloidal solution. Salt or sugar in water is a true solution.","e":"Other kitchen colloids: milk, butter, jelly, mayonnaise.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"An oil-and-vinegar dressing is an emulsion, which is also a kind of colloid; the key still marks coffee."},{"id":"q0731","q":"Sugar has the ability to absorb moisture from the surrounding it is exposed to. One of the following terms that describes this is known as ___________.","o":["Eroding","Condensation","Activation","Hygroscopy"],"a":3,"x":"Hygroscopy is a substance's ability to absorb moisture from the air; sugar and common salt are hygroscopic.","e":"Deliquescent substances absorb so much water that they dissolve, such as calcium chloride.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1301","q":"Which of the following elements has zero valency?","o":["Lithium","Beryllium","Helium","Fluorine"],"a":2,"x":"Helium, a noble gas, has a full outer shell (duet) and does not normally form bonds, so its valency is zero.","e":"Other noble gases: neon, argon, krypton, xenon, radon.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1077","q":"The modern Periodic Law is based on ________.","o":["Rutherford Scattering Law","Bohr’s Atomic Law","Octave Law","Moseley's Law"],"a":3,"x":"The modern periodic law (Moseley, 1913): properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic numbers, not atomic masses.","e":"Mendeleev's law was based on atomic mass; Newlands proposed the law of octaves.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1144","q":"A homogeneous mixture of two or more components is a solution. The component present in the largest quantity is generally known as the:","o":["solute","vapour","solvent","precipitate"],"a":2,"x":"In a solution, the component present in the largest amount is the solvent; the smaller components are solutes.","e":"In salt water, water is the solvent and salt the solute.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1175","q":"180 g of glucose (C6H12O6) is dissolved in 1 kg of water. At what temperature will water boil at 1.013 bar (Kb for water is 0.52K kg mol-1)?","o":["373.67 K","373.15 K","373.27 K","373.202 K"],"a":0,"x":"180 g of glucose is 1 mole; in 1 kg of water that is 1 molal. ΔTb = Kb × m = 0.52 × 1 = 0.52 K, so the boiling point = 373.15 + 0.52 = 373.67 K.","e":"Boiling-point elevation is a colligative property: it depends on the number of solute particles.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1188","q":"The concentration of a solution cannot be described by ___________.","o":["parts per million","volume by mass percentage (V/w)","mole fraction","mass by volume percentage (w/V)"],"a":1,"x":"Standard concentration terms include mass %, volume %, mass by volume % (w/V), ppm, mole fraction, molarity and molality. 'Volume by mass %' (V/w) is not used.","e":"ppm is used for very dilute solutions, such as fluoride in water.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1519","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct about halogens?","o":["This is the only group that contains elements in all three states of matter at room temperature and pressure.","Halogens are group 17th elements.","Chlorine is the most electronegative element.","This is a group of highly reactive non-metals."],"a":2,"x":"The incorrect statement is that chlorine is the most electronegative element. Fluorine is. The others hold: halogens are group 17, highly reactive non-metals, and occur as gas (F₂, Cl₂), liquid (Br₂) and solid (I₂).","e":"Chlorine has the highest electron affinity; fluorine the highest electronegativity.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1537","q":"Which law states that the partial pressure of a gas in the vapour phase is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas in the solution?","o":["Boyle's law","Charles's law","Henry's law","Raoult's law"],"a":2,"x":"Henry's law: the partial pressure of a gas in the vapour phase is proportional to its mole fraction in the solution (p = K_H·x) — gas solubility rises with pressure.","e":"That is why soft drinks are bottled under high CO₂ pressure.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]}]},{"id":"reactions","n":"Reactions, Acids and Bases","qs":[{"id":"q0309","q":"Which of the following is an example of a chemical change?","o":["Rusting of iron","Freezing water","Melting of ice","Boiling of water"],"a":0,"x":"Rusting of iron is a chemical change: iron reacts with oxygen and moisture to form a new substance, hydrated iron oxide. Freezing, melting and boiling are physical changes.","e":"Rusting can be prevented by galvanising (zinc coating), painting or oiling.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1606","q":"pH value of neutral water should be-","o":["9","6","7","8"],"a":2,"x":"Pure (neutral) water has a pH of 7 at 25°C.","e":"Below 7 is acidic (lemon juice ~2); above 7 is basic (soap ~10).","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0571","q":"Which among the following acids is also known as 'Muriatic acid'?","o":["Carbonic Acid","Sulphuric Acid","Hydrochloric Acid","Acetic Acid"],"a":2,"x":"Muriatic acid is the old commercial name for hydrochloric acid (HCl); our stomach makes HCl to digest food.","e":"Oil of vitriol: sulphuric acid. Aqua fortis: nitric acid.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1152","q":"turmeric gives red color when it reacts with _____________","o":["Base","Salt","Water","Acid"],"a":0,"x":"Turmeric is a natural indicator: it turns reddish-brown with a base, such as soap solution, and stays yellow in acid.","e":"Other natural indicators: litmus (from lichens), red cabbage, China rose.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1233","q":"According to Le Chatelier's principle, which conditions mainly shift the equilibrium of the Haber process towards ammonia formation?","o":["Low temperature and low pressure","Low pressure and high temperature with a catalyst","High pressure and low temperature","Low pressure and high temperature"],"a":2,"x":"N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ is exothermic and reduces the number of gas molecules, so high pressure and low temperature favour ammonia (Le Chatelier). In practice about 450°C, 200 atm and an iron catalyst are used.","e":"A catalyst speeds up the reaction but does not shift the equilibrium.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1510","q":"The letter 'p' in pH stands for ‘potenz’ which in German means __________.","o":["power","proton","potential","percent"],"a":0,"x":"In pH, the 'p' comes from the German 'potenz', meaning power (of hydrogen-ion concentration): pH = −log[H⁺].","e":"pH 7 is neutral; below 7 acidic; above 7 basic.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0864","q":"What is an aqueous solution containing a mixture of a weak acid and its salt, or a weak base and its salt, called?","o":["Hypotonic solution","Unsaturated solution","Buffer solution","Saturated solution"],"a":2,"x":"A buffer solution contains a weak acid and its salt (or a weak base and its salt) and resists changes in pH when a little acid or base is added.","e":"Blood is buffered at pH about 7.4 by the carbonic acid–bicarbonate system.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]}]},{"id":"everyday","n":"Chemistry in Everyday Life","qs":[{"id":"q0036","q":"Select the correct statement with reference to reducing sugars.","o":["All disaccharides are reducing sugars.","Reducing sugars do not react with Tollen's or Fehling's reagent.","Sucrose is a common example of a reducing sugar.","Reducing sugars can reduce mild oxidising agents like Fehling’s solution."],"a":3,"x":"Reducing sugars have a free aldehyde or ketone group, so they reduce mild oxidising agents such as Fehling's and Tollens' reagents. Glucose is one; sucrose is not.","e":"Glucose turns blue Fehling's solution into a brick-red precipitate.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0389","q":"Select the odd one with reference to carbohydrates.","o":["C6H12O6","C12H22O11","C6H12O5","CH3COOH"],"a":3,"x":"C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose), C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (sucrose) and C₆H₁₂O₅ (a deoxy sugar) are carbohydrates. CH₃COOH is acetic acid, a carboxylic acid, not a carbohydrate.","e":"Vinegar is about 5–8% acetic acid.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1294","q":"Which of the following is present in chalk?","o":["Sodium Hydroxide","Potassium Nitrate","Sodium Chloride","Calcium Carbonate"],"a":3,"x":"Chalk is calcium carbonate (CaCO₃); limestone and marble are also CaCO₃.","e":"Heating CaCO₃ gives quicklime (CaO) and CO₂.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0968","q":"What is the main acid component in vinegar?","o":["Citric acid","Acetic acid","Tannic acid","Nitric acid"],"a":1,"x":"Vinegar is a dilute solution (about 5–8%) of acetic (ethanoic) acid, CH₃COOH.","e":"Lemon has citric acid; tea has tannic acid; curd has lactic acid.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1258","q":"Pyrolusite ore (MnO2) is used for the preparation of _____.","o":["sodium chromate","potassium dichromate","potassium permanganate","sodium manganate"],"a":2,"x":"Pyrolusite (MnO₂) is the main manganese ore and is used to make potassium permanganate (KMnO₄).","e":"Potassium dichromate comes from chromite ore.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1480","q":"Which of the following acids is found in orange?","o":["Citric acid","Oxalic acid","Methanoic acid","Lactic acid"],"a":0,"x":"Oranges and lemons contain citric acid.","e":"Tomato: oxalic acid. Ant sting: methanoic (formic) acid. Curd: lactic acid.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q0839","q":"Which of the following acidic functional groups is present in picric acid?","o":["hydroxyl (-OH)","carboxylic acid (-COOH)","phosphoric acid (-PO4H2)","sulfonic acid (-SO3H) Q The Gandamak treaty was signed in 1879 between which of the following parties?"],"a":0,"x":"Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol) is a phenol: its acidic group is the hydroxyl (–OH), made strongly acidic by three nitro groups.","e":"It was used as an explosive and as a yellow dye.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]}]}]});
